                        "content": "🚀 Future Expressive Structures",
                        "level": 1,
                        "align": "left"
                    },
                    "state": "active",
                    "status": "uncompleted"
                },
                {
                    "id": "paragraph-1-1",
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "props": {
                        "content": "Unlike the past or present, English does not have a single verb ending for the future (like '-ed'). Instead, we choose from four different future structures depending on whether we are expressing a <strong>spontaneous decision</strong>, a <strong>prior plan</strong>, a <strong>fixed arrangement</strong>, or an <strong>official timetable</strong>.",
                        "align": "left"
                    },
                    "state": "active",
                    "status": "uncompleted"
                },
                {
                    "id": "poll-1-1",
                    "type": "poll",
                    "props": {
                        "question": "Which sentence expresses a SPONTANEOUS decision made right now?",
                        "options": [
                            {
                                "id": "opt1",
                                "text": "Hold on, I will open the door for you."
                            },
                            {
                                "id": "opt2",
                                "text": "I am going to open a business next summer."
                            },
                            {
                                "id": "opt3",
                                "text": "The train arrives at 4:00 PM."
